t.\n\nGiven the instruction \"Use your knowledge of Sanskrit nominal morphology\" and \"Do not simply infer the stem from the nominative singular form\", we are expected to know the stems. So I think I should output the stem as it is, and for stem_class, if it ends in a vowel, use the appropriate class; if it ends in a consonant, maybe we cannot use these classes. But the JSON schema requires one of those exact strings. So I have to pick one, perhaps the class of the vowel that appears in the stem's last syllable? But that would be inconsistent.\n\nMaybe there's a convention: In some systems, consonant stems are assigned a class based on the final consonant, e.g., \"C_STEM\". But it's not allowed. \n\nLet's check the full list of allowed values: A_STEM, AA_STEM, I_STEM, II_STEM, U_STEM, UU_STEM, R_STEM. That's it. The only stem ending in ṛ is R_STEM. So words like \"svasṛ\" end in ṛ, so R_STEM. Others like \"pitṛ\" would be R_STEM. So for words ending in other consonants, there is no class. So maybe all words in the list end in one of these vowels? Let's re-examine the so-called consonant stems:\n\n- parameṣṭhin: ends in -in, which is -i + n. So it ends in n. Not a vowel.\n- śakṛt: ends in t.\n- viṣ: ends in ṣ.\n- enas: ends in s.\n- aryaman: ends in n.\n- viśvakarman: ends in n.\n- sākṣin: ends in n.\n- dhanvan: ends in n.\n- plīhan: ends in n.\n- upasad: ends in d.\n- mahant: ends in t.\n- ūṣman: ends in n.\n\nNone end in the allowed vowels. So they are not covered. \n\nCould it be that these words are actually given in the nominative singular, and we need to strip the nominative ending to get the stem, which would then end in a vowel? For example, \"parameṣṭhin\": if it's nominative singular, it would be parameṣṭhī, not parameṣṭhin. So no.\n\nWhat about \"śakṛt\"? The nominative singular neuter is śakṛt, which looks like the stem. But sometimes neuter stems ending in -as have nom/acc sg in -aḥ? enas: nom/acc sg is enaḥ? Actually, neuter s-stems like manas have no