s look at \"cakrin\": this is an adjective meaning \"having wheels\", stem cakrin- (ending in -in). But it can also be cakrī in the nominative? The stem is cakrin, but the nominative singular masculine is cakrī. So the lemma might be given as \"cakrin\" which is the stem. That ends in -in, a consonant. But it could be considered as having a stem in -i if we follow a different analysis? In Sanskrit, stems in -in are considered as having the suffix -in, and they decline with strong and weak stems. The stem itself ends in -n. So not a vowel.\n\nOther examples: \"manthin\" - stem manthin-, meaning churning. Again, -in stem. \"kṣīrin\" - milky, stem kṣīrin-. \"svarasāman\" - stem svarasāman-, meaning \"having sweet sounds\"? That's a compound, stem sāman-. So it ends in -an. Not vowel.\n\n\"rumaṇvant\" - stem rumaṇvant-, meaning \"having the Rumaṇvant mountains\". Suffix -vant, so consonant.\n\n\"bhargas\" - stem bhargas-, neuter, meaning radiance. Ends in -as.\n\n\"pāthas\" - stem pāthas-, neuter, meaning water. Ends in -as.\n\n\"vār\" - stem vār-, neuter, meaning water. Ends in -r.\n\n\"mahābrahman\" - stem mahābrahman-, meaning \"great Brahman\". Ends in -an.\n\n\"barhiṣad\" - we discussed.\n\n\"jāgrant\" - present participle.\n\n\"niyut\" - stem niyut-, feminine, meaning a team of horses.\n\nSo there are many consonant stems. The instructions clearly say \"determine its underlying nominal stem and the vowel class of that stem.\" For consonant stems, there is no vowel class. So perhaps the intended output for such cases is to still output the stem, but the stem_class becomes something like \"CONSONANT\"? But the allowed values are only the seven. Maybe the \"stem_class\" is meant to be the class of the stem based on its ending, and for consonant stems, we might need to infer the stem's final vowel from its inflection? For example, for stems in -in, the stem is treated as if it ends in -i? In some grammatical traditions, they are considered as ending in -in, but the stem itself might be considered to have an inserable vowel? No.\n\nI'll check the initial examples: they are all clear vowel stems. Possibly the dataset is divided into clear vowel stems and some ambiguous ones. In many computational Sanskrit tools, stems are often given in their base form, and the stem_class is determined by the final character. For consonant stems, they would not fit into these classes. So maybe the person who created the instruction assumed all lemmas would be vowel stems. But the provided list clearly contains many consonant stems.\n\nMaybe I should interpret the task as: Given the nominative singular form, derive the stem, which will always end in one of the listed vowels. So for lemmas that appear to be consonant stems, they are actually the nominative singular form of a vowel stem. Let's test this: \"barhiṣad\" - could it be the nominative singular of a stem \"barhiṣada\"? No, that would be \"barhiṣadaḥ\" (masculine) or \"barhiṣadam\" (neuter). \"barhiṣad\" as nominative singular neuter? Actually, neuter nouns ending in -ad? The stem barhiṣad is neuter? MW says barhiṣad is mfn., so the nominative singular masculine is barhiṣat, feminine barhiṣatī, neuter barhiṣat. So \"barhiṣad\" is not a nominative form; it's the stem form before sandhi. So if the input is the stem, then it is a consonant stem. If we assume the input is nominative, \"barhiṣad\" would be wrong because nominative is barhiṣat. So likely the input is the stem.\n\nThus, the only way to reconcile is to allow consonant stems and somehow map them to one of the classes, perhaps based on their inflectional pattern? For example, stems in -as are neuter s-stems, and they might be considered as having a stem in -a? No, that's different. Stems in -as are distinct.\n\nGiven the instructions are strict, I'll output the stem as is (even if ending in consonant) and for stem_class, I'll put the class of the stem's final sound if it's a vowel, else I'll have to choose a default. But that would be wrong. Alternatively, maybe I can set confidence to LOW for those cases and make a best guess. The instruction says: \"If the lemma is ambiguous, irregular, or you are not sufficiently certain, give your best answer and set confidence to LOW.\" So for consonant stems, since we don't have a matching class, we can set confidence to LOW and perhaps assign a class based on the closest vowel? That seems arbitrary.\n\nMaybe I can look up the standard dictionary stem for each. Many of these consonant stems have alternative forms with vowels. For example, \"niyut\" is sometimes given as \"niyut\" but also as \"niyutā\" in some compounds? Actually, Apte gives \"niyut\" as a feminine noun, but it declines like a root noun? MW lists it as niyut-, but also notes that it is sometimes used as an epithet of Sarasvati? Possibly it's a variant. But I need a systematic approach.\n\nI'll consider that the stem_iast should be the base form without the nominative ending. For most vowel stems, the nominative singular is often the stem (for neuter a-stems, i- and u-stems, etc.) or adds a visarga (for masculine a-stems), etc. The given lemmas are in IAST, so they are likely the stem forms as they appear in dictionaries. In many cases, the dictionary form is the stem for nouns, but for some consonant stems, it might be the nominative? Typically, Sanskrit dictionaries give the stem. So we are given the stem. So we must output the stem_iast as exactly that stem, even if it ends in a consonant. Then the stem_class should indicate the vowel class of the stem. But since the stem doesn't end in a vowel, we can't. The instruction might have a bug: it says \"vowel class of that stem\", but it doesn't provide for consonant stems. So I'll assume that the intended stem_class for consonant stems is something like \"CONSONANT\", but since it's not allowed, maybe I can set it to \"A_STEM\" as a fallback? That would be inaccurate.\n\nLet's re-read: \"For every Sanskrit nominal lemma given in IAST, determ