ion says \"Return exactly one result for every input lemma.\" So we must return for all.\n\nGiven the time, I will assume that the intended stem_iast is the dictionary stem, and for stem_class, if the stem ends in a vowel from the list, use that class; if it ends in a consonant, I will set confidence to LOW and choose a default class, perhaps A_STEM, to be consistent, but note in the output? But I can't add notes.\n\nMaybe I can look up the gender and typical inflection to derive a thematic vowel. For example, many masculine and neuter stems in -a are A_STEM. For -ā, feminine. For -i, -u, etc. For consonant stems, they might be considered as having no vowel, but we need to output one of these. I could set stem_class to the class that corresponds to the stem's canonical ending if we were to make it a vowel stem? That seems arbitrary.\n\nPerhaps for the purpose of this exercise, we should treat all stems as ending in a vowel by taking the stem from the nominative singular form if it's given in a way that looks like a nominative? But many look like stems.\n\nLet's check a few: \"barhiṣad\" - if it were a nominative, it would be masculine/feminine? No, nominative singular of a root noun \"barhiṣad\" is barhiṣat. So it's not nominative. So it's the stem.\n\n\"jāgrant\" - nominative singular masculine of the present participle is jāgran, neuter jāgrat. The form jāgrant could be the stem before sandhi? Actually, the stem is jāgrant- (strong stem) which is used before vowels? In IAST, they might write jāgrant as the stem. So it's the stem.\n\nThus, I think the dataset includes both vowel and consonant stems. The instruction may have an oversight. Since I must output JSON, I'll do my best to identify the stem and the stem class. For consonant stems, I'll check if there is a homophonous vowel stem. If not, I'll set stem_iast to the stem as it appears (which might be the same as lemma_iast), and for stem_class, I'll pick one of the classes arbitrarily? No, I should use my know