tem. Actually, Monier-Williams often gives the stem for -ṛ stems. But here, we have to determine stem from the given form. Since the task says \"For every Sanskrit nominal lemma given in IAST, determine its underlying nominal stem\", the lemma is the dictionary form. We should recognize it as an R_STEM if it ends in -ṛ. So stem_iast: īśitṛ, stem_class: R_STEM. Confidence: HIGH.\n\n26. kaitava: ends in -a. Neuter (gambling). Stem: kaitava, A_STEM.\n\n27. kāraṇā: ends in -ā. Feminine. Stem: kāraṇā, AA_STEM.\n\n28. kaumudī: ends in -ī. Feminine. Stem: kaumudī, II_STEM. (Moonlight)\n\n29. kaupīna: ends in -a. Neuter. Stem: kaupīna, A_STEM.\n\n30. ketaka: ends in -a. Masculine. Stem: ketaka, A_STEM.\n\n31. kilakilā: ends in -ā. Feminine. Stem: kilakilā, AA_STEM? But note: kilakilā is often used as an indeclinable for sound? Actually, it's a feminine noun meaning \"sound of joy\". So AA_STEM. HIGH.\n\n32. kīnāśa: ends in -a. Masculine. Stem: kīnāśa, A_STEM.\n\n33. kriyāśakti: ends in -i. Feminine. Compound. Stem: kriyāśakti. I_STEM? Often abstract feminine nouns in -śakti are I_STEM. Confidence HIGH.\n\n34. kṣālana: ends in -a. Neuter. Stem: kṣālana, A_STEM.\n\n35. kṣīravṛkṣa: ends in -a. Masculine. Stem: kṣīravṛkṣa, A_STEM.\n\n36. kṣoṇī: ends in -ī. Feminine. Stem: kṣoṇī, II_STEM. Earth.\n\n37. kuṇḍalinī: ends in -ī. Feminine. Stem: kuṇḍalinī, II_STEM.\n\n38. kuruśreṣṭha: ends in -a. Masculine. Stem: kuruśreṣṭha, A_STEM.\n\n39. lekhana: ends in -a. Neuter. Stem: lekhana, A_STEM.\n\n40. lokeśa: ends in -a. Masculine. Stem: lokeśa, A_STEM.\n\n41. madhupa: ends in -a. Masculine (drunkard) or bee. Stem: madhupa, A_STEM.\n\n42. majjana: ends in -a. Neuter (sinking, bathing). Stem: majjana, A_STEM.\n\n43. mālikā: ends in -ā. Feminine. Stem: mālikā, AA_STEM.\n\n44. mallaka: ends in -a. Masculine/neuter. Stem: mallaka, A_STEM.\n\n45. mantharā: ends in -ā. Feminine? Name? Probably AA_STEM.\n\n46. maṭha: ends in -a. Masculine (monastery). Stem: maṭha, A_STEM.\n\n47. mūtrāghāta: en